WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.908
File uploads and user-generated content
require explicit design in Razor Pages

2
00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:08.213
applications.

3
00:00:08.293 --> 00:00:11.968
A file field on a form is not just another
input.

4
00:00:12.048 --> 00:00:17.984
It introduces size, storage, naming,
content review, download behavior,

5
00:00:18.064 --> 00:00:23.341
retention, access control, and operational
capacity questions.

6
00:00:23.421 --> 00:00:29.452
Those questions should be answered before
the feature reaches production.

7
00:00:29.532 --> 00:00:33.867
Review upload size limits, allowed
business purpose,

8
00:00:33.947 --> 00:00:39.695
accepted file categories, storage
location, generated storage names,

9
00:00:39.775 --> 00:00:45.617
scanning or review workflow, download
authorization, retention rules,

10
00:00:45.697 --> 00:00:47.488
and cleanup behavior.

11
00:00:47.568 --> 00:00:54.164
The page model should make the intended
flow visible rather than treating uploaded

12
00:00:54.244 --> 00:00:57.165
content as an ordinary string value.

13
00:00:57.245 --> 00:01:03.841
Uploaded files should not be stored where
they can be executed as application code.

14
00:01:03.921 --> 00:01:08.821
Storage should be separated from
application runtime paths,

15
00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:15.215
and download behavior should go through
access checks when files are private or

16
00:01:15.295 --> 00:01:16.614
user-specific.

17
00:01:16.694 --> 00:01:23.384
A safe design avoids giving uploaded
content more capability than the business need

18
00:01:23.464 --> 00:01:24.313
requires.

19
00:01:24.393 --> 00:01:28.633
Do not rely on file names, content types,
extensions,

20
00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:32.105
or user-supplied metadata by themselves.

21
00:01:32.185 --> 00:01:36.991
Those values can be useful hints for
usability and workflow,

22
00:01:37.071 --> 00:01:43.008
but the application should make decisions
using a reviewed combination of

23
00:01:43.088 --> 00:01:48.647
server-side naming, validation, storage
policy, content handling,

24
00:01:48.727 --> 00:01:50.329
and business rules.

25
00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:56.063
Static file configuration should expose
only intended public assets.

26
00:01:56.143 --> 00:02:02.174
It is easy to focus on upload pages and
forget that static file middleware can

27
00:02:02.254 --> 00:02:05.835
publish files from configured directories.

28
00:02:05.915 --> 00:02:10.626
Review which folders are public, which
files are excluded,

29
00:02:10.706 --> 00:02:14.287
whether directory behavior is appropriate,

30
00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:19.361
and how build or deployment output affects
published content.

31
00:02:19.441 --> 00:02:25.566
Large uploads can affect memory, disk,
network, queues, scanning services,

32
00:02:25.646 --> 00:02:27.437
and backend capacity.

33
00:02:27.517 --> 00:02:31.569
Limits and failure behavior should be
deliberate.

34
00:02:31.649 --> 00:02:36.643
A secure and reliable upload flow tells
users what is accepted,

35
00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:42.188
rejects unsupported content safely,
records operational signals,

36
00:02:42.268 --> 00:02:46.980
and gives administrators a way to manage
storage over time.
